there's a very interesting new study  called chronic low calorie sweetener use  and risk of abdominal obesity among  older adults this is by Chia char del  Tanaka and others and what this study  looked at was let's look at people who  use low-calorie sweeteners instead of  sugar and let's look at whether that's  actually something that correlates with  more effectively controlling weight  measured by abdominal obesity and what  the study did it was a longitudinal  study it looked at thousands and  thousands of measurements and food diary  records with detailed information on  sugar versus low calorie sweetener  consumption etc etc they had a median  follow-up time of 10 years I won't get  into every detail about the study but  needless to say we are linking to it and  you can check it out and what the study  determined was that low calorie  sweetener use was independently  associated with heavier relative weight  a larger waist and a higher prevalence  and incidence of abdominal obesity  suggesting that low calorie sweetener  use may not be an effective means of  weight control relative to sugar use now  number one correlation versus causation  one might read the study and say oh well  you use low calorie sweeteners instead  of sugar and you end up heavier or more  overweight that's not what the study is  saying what it's saying is that those  who use low calorie sweeteners instead  of sugar tend to have a higher BMI or be  more obese but that isn't necessarily  because of the sweetener use right and  the the study says that they controlled  for other variables because what we  really need to determine is are  individuals who are already heavier more  obese more likely to use low calorie  sweeteners than sugar but that's not  actually why they are obese so that's I  think a very very fair question but my  thing has always been Pat use a little  sugar or get used to not using sugar so  like for example with coffee  I used to put quite a bit of sugar in my  coffee and my mom for example uses a ton  of artificial sweetener and I said you  know it doesn't really make sense to me  I think I'm just gonna start putting in  less less sugar into my coffee and I got  used to that and now I don't drink  coffee with sugar at all that seems more  logical to me than just making things  super sweet with artificial sweeteners  which many many of which have been shown  to not be healthy I think a lot of  people just just go to these options  things like diet soda Splenda equal  because they think it's the healthier  operative yeah and their doctors may  tell them to embrace a low sugar diet  and they think well you know I don't  have I don't have to give up these  drinks that I like I can still continue  to have them it can still be yeah no  doubt about it and we saw this  phenomenon with the so called low-fat  snack craze where you had cookies that  were low fat but instead had other  chemicals in them where you saw other  types of snacks that were low-fat potato  chips that were low fat etc etc number  one it's unclear that the replacement of  those fats with chemicals and other  substances is not worse for you and  additionally we know that people tend to  over indulge in foods that they perceive  to be healthier so yeah I don't remember  if it was snack Wells or what experiment  was done so I'm kind of low-fat cookie  and people would just eat way way more  because they were like hey it's low-fat  I can eat twice as much yeah I think  it's more of a marketing thing than in  any interest in protecting people's  health yep I totally agree with you and  very very similar findings bottom line  if you're having a weight issue it's far  healthier to for example ditch the soda  altogether than switching from two cokes  a day to do diet cokes a day if you are  looking to reduce the amount of sugar  you're consuming I would say get used to  stuff being not as sweet as opposed to  switching to equal or Splenda or  whatever artificial sweetener it is that  you're using and the science does seem  to bear that out
 

